6. Understanding HTTP status codes

These are extremely important in DevOps.
200 — Success ✅

HTTP/2 200

Means:

    Everything worked.

Example:

curl https://myserver.com/api/health

Response:

{
  "status": "healthy"
}

Log:

10.0.0.15 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:30:01 +0530] "GET /api/health HTTP/1.1" 200 28

a) 301 — Permanently redirected

Suppose someone visits:

http://example.com

but the website wants everyone to use HTTPS.

The server might respond:

HTTP/1.1 301 Moved Permanently
Location: https://example.com/

You can investigate with:

curl -I http://example.com

You might get:

HTTP/1.1 301 Moved Permanently
Location: https://example.com/

Basically:

    "Don't use this address. Go over there instead."

b) 302 — Temporary redirect

Similar idea:

HTTP/1.1 302 Found
Location: /login

For example, you visit:

/dashboard

but you're not logged in.

The server might send you to:

/login

c) 404 — Not Found ❌

You run:

curl https://example.com/abc123

And get:

HTTP/2 404

This means:

    The server is working, but that particular resource doesn't exist.

Your log might contain:

10.0.0.20 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:35:10 +0530] "GET /abc123 HTTP/1.1" 404 153

As a DevOps engineer, you'd immediately notice the 404.

For example:

curl https://myserver.com/api/payment

Response:

HTTP/1.1 500 Internal Server Error

7. A realistic troubleshooting example

a) Imagine your customer says:

    "The website is down."

You don't immediately restart the server.

First:

curl -I https://mycompany.com

You get:

HTTP/2 200

Website is responding.

b) Then customer says:

    "Login isn't working."

You test:

curl -I https://mycompany.com/login

You get:

HTTP/2 200

So /login itself is reachable.

c) Now you check the application API:

curl -I https://mycompany.com/api/login

You get:

HTTP/2 500

8. Now look at the log

You open the access log:

tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log

tail -f means:

    Show me the end of the file and keep watching for new lines.

You might see:

10.0.0.15 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:40:01 +0530] "GET / HTTP/2.0" 200 1256
10.0.0.15 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:40:03 +0530] "GET /login HTTP/2.0" 200 3421
10.0.0.15 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:40:05 +0530] "POST /api/login HTTP/2.0" 500 721
10.0.0.15 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:40:06 +0530] "GET /favicon.ico HTTP/2.0" 404 153

This tells you:

/                  → 200 ✅
/login             → 200 ✅
/api/login         → 500 ❌
/favicon.ico       → 404 ⚠️

The important problem is probably:

POST /api/login → 500

9. Access log vs error log

This distinction is very important.

a) Access log

Usually tells you:

    Who requested what, when, and what status they got.

Example:

10.0.0.15 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:40:05 +0530] \
"POST /api/login HTTP/2.0" 500 721

b) Error log

Usually tells you:

    What went wrong on the server.

For example:

2026/09/01 12:40:05 [error] 1234#1234:
*567 upstream prematurely closed connection
while reading response header from upstream,
client: 10.0.0.15,
request: "POST /api/login HTTP/2.0",
upstream: "http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/login"

10. Useful commands to remember:

a) Watch an Nginx log live

tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log

b) Look at the last 50 log lines

tail -50 /var/log/nginx/access.log

c) Search for errors

grep "500" /var/log/nginx/access.log

d) Download a file

wget https://example.com/file.zip

e) Follow redirects with curl

curl -L https://example.com

f) Resume a partially downloaded file with wget

wget -c https://example.com/bigfile.zip

g) Test a website

curl https://example.com

h) Check only HTTP headers

curl -I https://example.com

i) See detailed connection information

curl -v https://example.com

11. The DevOps mental model

Try to remember this flow:

             curl / browser / wget
                     |
                     ↓
                Web Server
               (Nginx/Apache)
                     |
                     ↓
                Application
                     |
                     ↓
                  Database

When something fails, you work your way through the chain.

For example:

curl
 ↓
200 ✅
 ↓
Nginx
 ↓
500 ❌
 ↓
Check Nginx error log
 ↓
Check application log
 ↓
Check database

That's the basic idea behind troubleshooting with curl + logs.

