1. What about wget?

This is where things get interesting.

wget is primarily designed for retrieving/downloading resources, so GET is its normal use case:

wget https://example.com/file.zip

That's essentially:

GET /file.zip

You can also see the request with:

wget -S https://example.com/file.zip

Example:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/zip
Content-Length: 10485760

Saving to: 'file.zip'

2. Can wget do POST?

Yes.

For example:

wget --post-data='name=Rahul&age=30' \
     https://example.com/users

Here you're sending data using POST.
